text:
A Dix Hills man was indicted Wednesday on multiple charges involving a car crash that left an older couple severely injured, Suffolk County District Attorney Ray Tierney announced. Miandad Khan, 37, of Dix Hills, was indicted for Aggravated Vehicular Assault,
Driving While Impaired by a Drug, and other related charges, after he allegedly entered into the
opposite lanes of travel at high speeds on Deer Park Avenue under the influence of drugs causing
a multi vehicle crash in May. “One allegedly impaired driver hurt four people and caused crashes involving six vehicles. These
charges reflect the severity of the defendant’s alleged actions and my office’s commitment to
protecting the public from dangerous drivers,” Tierney said. “Impaired driving will not be tolerated.” According to court documents and the investigation, on May 17, at approximately
3:20 p.m., Miandad Khan was operating his silver 2016 Mercedes Benz E350 northbound on Deer
Park Avenue in Deer Park when he allegedly struck a white 2020 Acura near the intersection of
Deer Park Avenue and Jacqueline Way and then fled. During his flight, Khan allegedly drove
northbound over the center median into the southbound lanes of travel and accelerated at high
speeds. As he neared the intersection of Deer Park Avenue and Grand Boulevard, he allegedly struck a
Honda SUV occupied by two teenage brothers, causing their vehicle to spin out. Khan’s vehicle
continued northbound through the intersection when he allegedly crashed head on into a Toyota
Rav 4 occupied by a 70-year-old couple, causing both vehicles to slam into other cars. The couple
had to be extracted from their vehicle before being taken to Good Samaritan Hospital to undergo
surgery for facial and spinal fractures. Additional vehicles were damaged by debris. The brothers in the Honda SUV, as well as the driver of the 2020 Acura, were all taken to
Good Samaritan Hospital where they received medical treatment. Khan was arraigned Wednesday on the indictment before Supreme Court Justice John B.
Collins for the following charges:
 One count of Aggravated Vehicular Assault, a Class C felony;
 Two counts of Assault in the Second Degree, Class D violent felonies;
 One count of Vehicular Assault in the First Degree, a Class D felony;
 Two counts of Vehicular Assault in the Second Degree, Class E felonies;
 One count of Driving While Ability Impaired by Drugs, and unclassified misdemeanor;
 One count of Assault in the Third Degree, Class A misdemeanors;
 One count of Reckless Endangerment in the Second Degree, a Class A misdemeanor;
 One count of Reckless Driving, an unclassified misdemeanor;
 One count of Leaving the Scene of an Incident Without Reporting, a violation;
 One count of Speeding, a traffic infraction; and
 One count of Failure to Maintain Lane, a traffic infraction. Collins ordered Khan to be held on $100,000 cash, $200,000 bond or $1,000,000 partially
secured bond. Khan is due back in court on Oct.1, and faces 8 1/3 to 25 years in prison if convicted on the top count. 2 Huntington Residents Involved in Fatal Crash in Deer Park
